    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Acute Myeloid Leukemia (AML) is one of the most challenging cancers to treat, with current therapies yielding a 5-year survival rate of approximately 70% for pediatric patients and 50% for adult patients. Even when curative, current therapies are highly toxic and can cause life-threatening acute and chronic toxicities. The poor overall survival associated with current regimens creates an impetus to develop more effective and less toxic therapies. \r\n\tPrevious studies have reported that CREB (cyclic AMP Response Element Binding protein), a transcription factor critical for hematopoietic cell survival and proliferation, is overexpressed in the majority of AML patients and is an independent predictor of poor clinical outcome. RSK (Ribosomal S6 Kinase), the primary activator of CREB in AML cells, additionally plays an independent role in preventing hematopoietic cell death, although the overall role of RSK signaling in AML cells is unknown. In this study, we explore a novel strategy for AML therapy by blocking the RSK-CREB signaling axis using small molecule inhibitors. \r\n\tCREB inhibition induced cell cycle arrest and apoptosis of AML cells by downregulating CREB-driven genes and signaling pathways that promote AML cell survival. In vivo studies demonstrated that treatment with the CREB inhibitor prolonged survival in mouse models of AML without toxicity. Resistance to the CREB inhibitor was mediated by RSK activation and subsequent increased CREB phosphorylation. Independent blockade of RSK resulted in reduced AML cell survival in vitro, and AML cell lines with RSK knockdown exhibited decreased proliferation in vivo. Our results further define the role of CREB and RSK in AML and suggest that the RSK-CREB signaling pathway represents a potential therapeutic target for AML, laying the groundwork for development of a new family of chemotherapy drugs.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
